WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:12.110
So last time, we talked a little bit about
the state of the world of belief, I suppose,

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:17.500
by the end of the nineteenth century and I
talked to you a little about Nietzsche, and

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:21.130
Dostoevsky, and Kierkegaard.

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:29.789
Um, a very large number of the clinical theories
that we’re going to be discussing for another

00:00:29.789 --> 00:00:36.130
two lectures after this one have been influenced
by philosophers, and that's partly why I’m

00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:39.110
also talking to you about the philosophers.

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:48.850
It’s almost as if in some sense the great
philosophers have - are tapped directly into

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:57.760
the lower strata of our cultural systems of
meaning as they move forward in time, and

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:03.510
they can outline what those structures are
and also describe their weaknesses, and their

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:08.120
strengths, and their likely transformations
moving forward.

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:15.350
You know, I mean obviously people can be behind
the times, and it’s just as probable that

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:20.399
some people are ahead of the times and we
would assume, if you're thinking about it,

00:01:20.399 --> 00:01:25.820
say, from a Big Five trait perspective that
the people who are ahead of the times often

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:30.979
are very, very intelligent and very, very
open, and of course that's a pretty good definition

00:01:30.979 --> 00:01:34.270
of a philosopher.

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:43.210
So, Nietzsche and Dostoevsky, more than any
other two thinkers had their finger on the

00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:53.170
pulse of the transmutation of cultural systems
of meaning at the end of the nineteenth century,

00:01:53.170 --> 00:02:01.209
and they were both concerned about the fact
that the systems of meaning within which Western

00:02:01.209 --> 00:02:09.709
civilization at least had taken shape, had
been fragmented for a variety of reasons,

00:02:09.709 --> 00:02:17.270
the most important of which was likely the
rise of the scientific worldview, and as I

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:23.970
detailed to you in the last lecture, both
Dostoevsky and Nietzsche believed that that

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:29.420
had left modern people because I think in
the early twentieth century, it was Western

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:35.970
people but I now think it’s reasonable to
say that it’s modern people in a state of

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:41.210
- they're - of cultural discontinuity.

00:02:41.210 --> 00:02:48.530
The things - belief systems but also technological
systems now have transformed the world so

00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:55.430
radically that the conflict between modernism
and traditional forms of belief is acutely

00:02:55.430 --> 00:02:57.450
felt everywhere.

00:02:57.450 --> 00:03:06.100
It’s certainly, I think, one of the forces
that have given rise to the battle between

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:13.620
Islamic fundamentalism and modernism because
it’s more than a battle of Islamic fundamentalism

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:21.050
against the Western world, and the problem,
fundamentally, is that people typically exist

00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:27.480
within cultural constructs that are very,
very ancient, and that have - that are grounded

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:34.840
in evolved systems of meaning that are even
deeper than the ones that are articulated

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:42.060
and explicit, and when there's a period of
very, very rapid cognitive and technological

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:50.990
change, then the integrity of those systems
starts to… it starts to become questionable

00:03:50.990 --> 00:03:57.620
from an explicit perspective but it also starts
to become insufficient from a practical perspective.

00:03:57.620 --> 00:04:04.560
So, one example that you might give some consideration
to is the fact of the introduction of the

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:07.080
birth control pill, for example.

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:16.280
If you listen to politically minded people,
they make the case that the emancipation of

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:21.820
women was essentially a political matter,
but I don't think that's a very reasonable

00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:23.729
way of looking at it at all.

00:04:23.729 --> 00:04:30.490
Of course, everything feeds back onto itself
and there are multiple causal pathways to

00:04:30.490 --> 00:04:32.370
any end.

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:39.139
The radical element in the emancipation of
women was of course the development of efficient

00:04:39.139 --> 00:04:45.229
forms of contraception, most particularly
the birth control pill, and it’s the case

00:04:45.229 --> 00:04:51.060
that as soon as you educate women anywhere
in the world, not only does economic productivity

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:55.740
arise dramatically, in fact it’s actually
the best predictor of increasing economic

00:04:55.740 --> 00:04:59.860
productivity in a modernizing state is the
granting of rights to women.

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:05.999
Now whether it’s directly because of the
granting of rights to women or because of

00:05:05.999 --> 00:05:11.319
the existence of an underlying belief structure
that allows the concept of right to expand

00:05:11.319 --> 00:05:17.291
quite rapidly is a very difficult to be sure
of, but in any case that exists, and then

00:05:17.291 --> 00:05:23.020
of course the other thing that happens is
that women stop having children unbelievably

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:24.310
rapidly.

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:31.479
So for example, I think - I believe it’s
Iran where this has been most marked over

00:05:31.479 --> 00:05:37.460
the last two generations, so from family sizes
far greater than replacement to family sizes

00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:41.139
well below replacement in basically one or
two generations.

00:05:41.139 --> 00:05:45.220
It’s a massive transformation, and it’s
not necessarily something that anyone expected.

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:53.749
It’s part of the sequence of forces that
are rapidly allowing our population to reach

00:05:53.749 --> 00:05:56.029
a peak and either stabilize or decline.

00:05:56.029 --> 00:06:00.419
Now I don't know how many of you know this,
but the projections are there’ll be nine

00:06:00.419 --> 00:06:05.240
billion people on the Earth within thirty
to fifty years, and that’ll be our peak

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:06.319
population.

00:06:06.319 --> 00:06:10.860
After that it will fall rapidly, and you know,
you can see that sort of thing happening right

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:14.650
now all over the Western world, and it’s
happening a lot in China, and it’s happening

00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:18.699
a lot in Japan where, you know, there are
more older people than there are younger people

00:06:18.699 --> 00:06:23.580
because the younger people aren't having enough
children to actually replace the population.

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:28.050
So the probability that we’ll peak at about
nine billion and then start to fall, it’s

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:32.939
not certain but that’s what the best projections
seem to indicate now.

00:06:32.939 --> 00:06:39.870
So what happens anyways with in periods of
very rapid technological and cognitive transformation

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:45.999
because those things go together that the
certainties upon which people base their interpretation

00:06:45.999 --> 00:06:54.480
of the world and even more importantly, base
their judgements about how to act start to

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:56.330
become uncertain.

00:06:56.330 --> 00:07:01.650
So for example, if you introduce the birth
control pill into a population, and you put

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:08.849
women’s reproductive faculties under their
own voluntary choice, then you radically transform

00:07:08.849 --> 00:07:15.320
virtually all of society’s fundamental social
structures, not least marriage, and marriage

00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:23.150
of course has been regarded classically as
the foundation of civilization, and of course,

00:07:23.150 --> 00:07:28.529
increasingly there are more people who aren't
married than there are people who are married.

00:07:28.529 --> 00:07:34.080
Now, it’s very difficult to know what to
make of that because of course there's no

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:41.069
setting the clock back, and it’s not even
clear that you would want to set back the

00:07:41.069 --> 00:07:47.529
clock if you could, but expecting cultural
constructs, which take centuries or maybe

00:07:47.529 --> 00:07:52.560
even thousands or maybe even tens of thousands
of years to develop, to keep up with change

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:56.520
of that magnitude is...it’s not possible.

00:07:56.520 --> 00:08:03.169
You know, I mean and you guys face technological
transformations that are earth-shattering

00:08:03.169 --> 00:08:06.169
on an almost yearly basis, and you hardly
even notice it.

00:08:06.169 --> 00:08:09.550
I mean, Tinder is a good example of that,
you know.

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:12.550
I don’t know if you know, but Tinder has
produced quite a spike in sexually transmitted

00:08:12.550 --> 00:08:17.379
diseases, but you know, it’s a radical technology
because it’s the first technology that’s

00:08:17.379 --> 00:08:24.210
ever been invented that enables men to find
partners with no fear - with virtually no

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:30.119
fear of rejection, and of course that's been
a limiting factor for… that’s been a defining

00:08:30.119 --> 00:08:35.750
feature between the interactions between men
and women ever since history began, and so

00:08:35.750 --> 00:08:42.880
these things are occurring at an extraordinary
rate, and of course it’s not reasonable

00:08:42.880 --> 00:08:48.690
to expect our more slower moving cultural
constructs to keep up with them, and that’s

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:53.610
partly because as well that, you know, people
talk for example about the divisive nature

00:08:53.610 --> 00:08:54.610
of religion.

00:08:54.610 --> 00:08:59.350
You often hear people who are critics of formal
religion in particular talk about the fact

00:08:59.350 --> 00:09:05.040
that religion underlies a tremendous amount
of destruction and warfare and conflict, and

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:09.699
you know, first of all it isn't religion that
does that by the way, it’s tribalism, and

00:09:09.699 --> 00:09:14.410
tribalism characterizes even chimpanzees,
and I don't think chimpanzees go to war with

00:09:14.410 --> 00:09:16.510
each other for religious reasons.

00:09:16.510 --> 00:09:23.040
So, you know, the religious groupings of mankind
are large-scale manifestations of the same

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:28.920
phenomena that produce dominance hierarchies
in the wild, and of course, large-scale religions

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:34.120
unite people within the religion just as much
as they divide people on the outside of it,

00:09:34.120 --> 00:09:40.620
so part of the accumulation of religious tradition
across time is a process that allows thousands,

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:46.620
and ten thousands, and even millions of people
to exist within the same hierarchy of values

00:09:46.620 --> 00:09:49.149
and exist relatively peacefully as a consequence.

00:09:49.149 --> 00:09:52.470
Now I’m saying relatively peacefully.

00:09:52.470 --> 00:10:00.330
You know, a hundred years ago, it was thought
that pre, let's say archaic people, so those

00:10:00.330 --> 00:10:04.940
would be people who are still living a fairly
isolated tribal life in small groups - pre

00:10:04.940 --> 00:10:09.529
- you know, basically operating at the level
of stone age technology, let’s say.

00:10:09.529 --> 00:10:16.250
The idea was - there was a very popular idea
for a long time that those cultures were communistic

00:10:16.250 --> 00:10:19.620
and violence free, and that's wrong.

00:10:19.620 --> 00:10:20.910
They're not violence free at all.

00:10:20.910 --> 00:10:25.420
If you track the homicide rates in stone-age
cultures, they're way, way higher than they

00:10:25.420 --> 00:10:30.480
are in civilized cultures, like orders of
magnitudes higher, and there's a variety of

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:35.041
reasons for that, but I’m just telling you
that because you want to dispense with the

00:10:35.041 --> 00:10:40.750
idea that along with complex civilizations
and the spread, say, of unifying religious

00:10:40.750 --> 00:10:45.230
beliefs, there was an increase in baseline
violence because there's just no evidence

00:10:45.230 --> 00:10:46.339
for that at all.

00:10:46.339 --> 00:10:52.550
Now, the problem with these large-scale belief
systems is they're not very fast, and part

00:10:52.550 --> 00:11:01.139
of the reason for that is that in order for
a large-scale belief system to manifest itself

00:11:01.139 --> 00:11:08.389
in any reasonable form, it has to be predicated
on the mutual agreement of the people within

00:11:08.389 --> 00:11:14.690
who...within - who operate within its embrace,
you know.

00:11:14.690 --> 00:11:21.399
So, for Toronto to exist as city, as a peaceful
city, basically what has to happen is that

00:11:21.399 --> 00:11:27.350
the vast majority of us have to agree that
the rules that govern the city, and the social

00:11:27.350 --> 00:11:34.319
interactions within the city are useful and
just, and because if you don't agree with

00:11:34.319 --> 00:11:39.860
that, then splinter movements of all sorts
start to occur and people become more revolutionary

00:11:39.860 --> 00:11:43.819
in their modes of action and then they become
more violent and the whole system starts to

00:11:43.819 --> 00:11:44.819
break down.

00:11:44.819 --> 00:11:52.040
So it’s not easy to establish a collective
norm because people have to agree to it, and

00:11:52.040 --> 00:11:57.209
you can imagine that hammering out agreement
with anything, with even a small agreement

00:11:57.209 --> 00:12:01.820
that affects many, many people, is a process
that takes a tremendous amount of time.

00:12:01.820 --> 00:12:07.040
People can argue forever about the smallest
alterations in the systems that regulate our

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:08.040
behavior.

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:13.260
Now, you know, some of you might be familiar
with the terror management theories.

00:12:13.260 --> 00:12:15.910
How many of you have heard of terror management
theory?

00:12:15.910 --> 00:12:21.150
Okay, well the terror management theories
are predicated on the idea that our belief

00:12:21.150 --> 00:12:24.140
systems protect us from our fear of death.

00:12:24.140 --> 00:12:28.570
Now exactly how they do that isn't specified
particularly well in the terror management

00:12:28.570 --> 00:12:33.960
theories but the originator of the theory
- his name was Ernest Becker who was a sociologist,

00:12:33.960 --> 00:12:42.770
by the way, and a Freudian - believed that
cultural systems enabled us to attribute beliefs

00:12:42.770 --> 00:12:49.129
to our actions, finite and infinite, so that
we could consider ourselves in relationship

00:12:49.129 --> 00:12:54.629
to mortality and in some sense, hide from
the truth of the finitude of our existence.

00:12:54.629 --> 00:12:59.800
Now, one of the things that terror management
theorists don't really give any credence to

00:12:59.800 --> 00:13:05.760
is the fact that belief systems are not only
systems of beliefs, they're systems of action

00:13:05.760 --> 00:13:07.639
regulation, right.

00:13:07.639 --> 00:13:11.320
So we talked about the Piagetian notion of
a game.

00:13:11.320 --> 00:13:17.560
I mean, the game exists first as something
that everyone can play and only later as something

00:13:17.560 --> 00:13:21.550
that people represent, and a social culture’s
the same way.

00:13:21.550 --> 00:13:22.629
A cultural…

00:13:22.629 --> 00:13:27.149
A culture regulates the way that you interact
with each other, and what you expect from

00:13:27.149 --> 00:13:32.949
each, and how you can fulfill your mutual
- your needs in relationship to one another

00:13:32.949 --> 00:13:38.569
and then it’s represented, and the representation
might help you find meaning in your life,

00:13:38.569 --> 00:13:43.480
but the fact of the initial social contract,
which is the phenomena that regulates your

00:13:43.480 --> 00:13:48.269
interpersonal behavior, doesn't protect you
from the fear of death, it protects you from

00:13:48.269 --> 00:13:51.940
dying, and that's a very important thing to
note.

00:13:51.940 --> 00:13:56.391
I mean, you know, all of you know, of course,
that it’s very cold out today, and yet here

00:13:56.391 --> 00:14:01.899
you are in this classroom where it’s, you
know, ridiculously comfortable, you know,

00:14:01.899 --> 00:14:05.870
by classroom standards and of course by standards
around the world.

00:14:05.870 --> 00:14:11.050
You're not freezing to death in here, and
that’s not your belief that’s protecting

00:14:11.050 --> 00:14:12.050
you.

00:14:12.050 --> 00:14:16.980
It’s the fact that you're embedded in this
insanely complicated system of cultural interactions

00:14:16.980 --> 00:14:21.199
and it just so happens that you get to sit
here and listen to a lecture while there's

00:14:21.199 --> 00:14:26.029
thousands of people beetling around, many
of them outside in the cold, making sure that

00:14:26.029 --> 00:14:30.529
the power grid, for example, that keeps this
place warm is properly maintained and functional

00:14:30.529 --> 00:14:33.840
which takes a tremendous amount of work.

00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:39.620
So, in during periods of rapid transformation,
it’s hard for the social contract to adjust

00:14:39.620 --> 00:14:43.910
itself so that everyone knows how to behave
in relationship to one another, but then it’s

00:14:43.910 --> 00:14:49.120
also very difficult for the description of
that, so the articulated norms that constitute

00:14:49.120 --> 00:14:54.180
a society; it’s very difficult of them to
transform rapidly enough so that they can

00:14:54.180 --> 00:15:02.440
keep track of the changes and help people
decide what they should do, so let me give

00:15:02.440 --> 00:15:03.440
you an example.

00:15:03.440 --> 00:15:09.360
So, I had a client a while back who had been
raised as a fundamentalist Christian, and

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:17.870
she was very… she had been socialized and
had come to believe that sex before marriage

00:15:17.870 --> 00:15:23.360
was wrong, but the probability that she was
going to get married before she was 27, or

00:15:23.360 --> 00:15:26.529
28, was low for a whole variety of reasons.

00:15:26.529 --> 00:15:30.519
So then she faced this conundrum, and it was
an interesting conundrum from my perspective

00:15:30.519 --> 00:15:40.439
because, you know, I think that in a well-regulated
psyche, sexuality is integrated into the personality

00:15:40.439 --> 00:15:45.839
so that it plays its role in the - what would
you call - in the polity of the self.

00:15:45.839 --> 00:15:52.199
It’s integrated properly inside, and so
it’s under moral control, roughly speaking

00:15:52.199 --> 00:15:58.360
because it serves its own function plus the
function of keeping the person well situated

00:15:58.360 --> 00:16:03.040
in the present and developing properly in
the future, and maintaining proper relationships

00:16:03.040 --> 00:16:08.060
with everyone around them, but it was quite
obvious to me that a lot of the constraints

00:16:08.060 --> 00:16:12.249
that had been placed on her behavior, as a
consequence of her relatively rigid belief

00:16:12.249 --> 00:16:16.770
were actually interfering with her development
as a person, you know, and so one of the things

00:16:16.770 --> 00:16:23.600
we had to puzzle out was well, exactly what
are the moral guidelines that you should use

00:16:23.600 --> 00:16:28.269
to regulate your sexual behavior outside of
marriage if you're not planning to be married

00:16:28.269 --> 00:16:30.079
for, you know, maybe until your late twenties.

00:16:30.079 --> 00:16:33.430
Well, you know good luck trying to figure
that out.

00:16:33.430 --> 00:16:38.079
Like it’s a really - it’s a really, really,
really, really complicated question, and it’s

00:16:38.079 --> 00:16:43.800
certainly not obvious that any one person
can figure out the answer to that in a single

00:16:43.800 --> 00:16:48.980
lifetime, you know, even if they thought of
nothing else especially because the landscape

00:16:48.980 --> 00:16:54.970
itself is transforming as you're attempting
to adjust to it, and you know, we eventually

00:16:54.970 --> 00:16:59.350
concluded - although it was a very individual
solution - we eventually concluded that there

00:16:59.350 --> 00:17:04.750
were things that she had forbidden herself
to do that were stopping her from establishing

00:17:04.750 --> 00:17:09.400
any kind of long term relationship at all,
and that was interfering with her development,

00:17:09.400 --> 00:17:14.199
you know, as a mature person, and that the
morality that she had used to structure her

00:17:14.199 --> 00:17:19.520
behavior appeared to be counterproductive
at least in, you know, some - in some areas.

00:17:19.520 --> 00:17:26.819
So, it’s one thing to, you know, to regulate
sexuality in the hopes of marriage when you

00:17:26.819 --> 00:17:29.740
get married when you're, you know, 19 or 20
or 21.

00:17:29.740 --> 00:17:32.779
It’s a completely different thing, perhaps,
when it’s not going to happen until you're

00:17:32.779 --> 00:17:34.870
in your late twenties.

00:17:34.870 --> 00:17:42.169
So now, Nietzsche talked a lot, and Dostoevsky
talked a lot about the collapse of meaning

00:17:42.169 --> 00:17:48.000
systems in the late nineteenth century, and
you know, that was followed by a very, very

00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:53.020
rapid period of technological transformation,
like that really kicked in in the late 1800’s,

00:17:53.020 --> 00:17:58.120
which was the, you know, well it was the height
of the industrial revolution, particularly

00:17:58.120 --> 00:18:04.380
in England, and there were modern technologies
being thrust out of the industrial revolution

00:18:04.380 --> 00:18:08.740
like mad, like the automobile, and the airplane,
and the electrical light, and the recording

00:18:08.740 --> 00:18:17.440
devices, and all the things that we’re still
elaborating on now, and then of course, apart

00:18:17.440 --> 00:18:23.030
from the collapse of classic, say, Christian
belief, and the introduction of all these

00:18:23.030 --> 00:18:28.000
new technologies, when World War I hit, the
entire monarchical structure of the Western

00:18:28.000 --> 00:18:33.029
world collapsed and that also occurred, say,
with regards to the Ottoman Empire, and that

00:18:33.029 --> 00:18:38.200
was partly what led to the creation of the
modern Middle East, and of course, that still

00:18:38.200 --> 00:18:41.100
hasn't been sorted out to any greater degree
at all.

00:18:41.100 --> 00:18:46.060
So the monarchical structures collapsed, these
ancient civilizations.

00:18:46.060 --> 00:18:52.400
The Russians underwent their revolution, and
were transformed into Communists, and then

00:18:52.400 --> 00:19:00.620
after World War I, the stress between the
potential different ways of structuring societies

00:19:00.620 --> 00:19:05.970
after the monarchical societies had collapsed
was almost unbearable, and people didn't really

00:19:05.970 --> 00:19:07.780
know what to do.

00:19:07.780 --> 00:19:15.029
Now, what happened at the end of Nietzsche’s
period and Dostoevsky’s period was that

00:19:15.029 --> 00:19:18.910
the question that both of those people...what?

00:19:18.910 --> 00:19:26.450
The most important question both of those
people asked became the central focus of the

00:19:26.450 --> 00:19:29.910
development of philosophical idea of ethics
in the twentieth century.

00:19:29.910 --> 00:19:33.360
So you could think about those - about that
as a post-religious ethic.

00:19:33.360 --> 00:19:38.660
Now, the reason I’m telling you this is
because, among other things, is because a

00:19:38.660 --> 00:19:47.030
lot of what psychoanalytic, or psychotherapeutic
treatment is about, is about ethics, and that

00:19:47.030 --> 00:19:50.870
can't be stretched too much because ethics
is about how you see the world and how you

00:19:50.870 --> 00:19:58.690
behave, and so even behaviorists who are technically
embedded in the scientific world are still

00:19:58.690 --> 00:20:05.570
practical ethicists because what they're consistently
doing with their clients is breaking down

00:20:05.570 --> 00:20:10.409
whatever the problems are that are causing
them misery, breaking them down into subproblems

00:20:10.409 --> 00:20:15.230
and trying to figure out solutions that improve
their quality of life, like practical, implementable

00:20:15.230 --> 00:20:20.350
solutions that improve their quality of life
not only now, but as they propagate into the

00:20:20.350 --> 00:20:22.710
future, and that is not a scientific issue.

00:20:22.710 --> 00:20:25.270
It’s a how to live issue.

00:20:25.270 --> 00:20:32.340
So, the entire history of the twentieth century,
in some sense, political, economic, psychological,

00:20:32.340 --> 00:20:40.370
was a sequence of attempts to answer the question
“When your fundamental systems of ethics

00:20:40.370 --> 00:20:42.880
collapse, how is is that you should live?”

00:20:42.880 --> 00:20:48.890
Now, Nietzsche said, very clearly, that there’d
be two consequences to the collapse of these

00:20:48.890 --> 00:20:54.309
systems, and one would be nihilism, the belief
in nothing at all, which also regarded as

00:20:54.309 --> 00:20:59.960
a form of escape from responsibility, so it’s
a logical consequence of the breakdown of

00:20:59.960 --> 00:21:05.110
classical belief systems, but it’s also
a cop-out and it’s the kind of cop-out that

00:21:05.110 --> 00:21:10.700
Dostoevsky explored very deeply in his small,
brilliant novel, “Notes from Underground”

00:21:10.700 --> 00:21:16.409
which describes a person who’s basically
slipped -- an intelligent person, and an irresponsible

00:21:16.409 --> 00:21:22.350
person, who has basically slipped into a pit
of meaninglessness where he experiences hatred

00:21:22.350 --> 00:21:26.460
and resentment and the desire for revenge,
and all of the sorts of things that would

00:21:26.460 --> 00:21:32.390
-- that afflict someone in the underworld
who’s got nothing to hold them together,

00:21:32.390 --> 00:21:36.730
and then of course, Nietzsche also talked
about the likelihood that people would turn

00:21:36.730 --> 00:21:43.320
to totalitarian belief systems, and he particularly
discussed Communism as a replacement for religious

00:21:43.320 --> 00:21:44.649
belief.

00:21:44.649 --> 00:21:47.260
I can give you a Canadian example of that.

00:21:47.260 --> 00:21:50.500
So, I heard a Gallup pollster one time.

00:21:50.500 --> 00:21:51.980
I was at a conference in Ottawa.

00:21:51.980 --> 00:21:57.250
It’s the only time I’ve ever heard this,
and I think it’s an amazing -- it’s an

00:21:57.250 --> 00:21:58.250
amazing fact.

00:21:58.250 --> 00:22:05.130
They were looking at the probability that
people would be separatists in Quebec, and

00:22:05.130 --> 00:22:09.440
if you were a lapsed Catholic, the probability
that you would be a Separatist was increased

00:22:09.440 --> 00:22:15.169
ten times, and the reason for that is, you
know, Catholicism fell apart in Quebec in

00:22:15.169 --> 00:22:16.169
the late 1950s.

00:22:16.169 --> 00:22:24.940
It was one of the last places in the Western
world, roughly speaking, where the feudal,

00:22:24.940 --> 00:22:29.640
in some sense, structures of Catholicism had
maintained themselves right up to that point,

00:22:29.640 --> 00:22:35.860
right up to the 1950s, the late 1950s, and
that collapsed precipitously just like belief

00:22:35.860 --> 00:22:41.279
in Christianity had in Russia, you know, in
the late, say, 1880s, and what happened in

00:22:41.279 --> 00:22:43.880
Quebec was, well first of all, the birth rate
plummeted.

00:22:43.880 --> 00:22:49.380
I mean, I did genetic research in Quebec,
and in the 1950s and before that, it was very

00:22:49.380 --> 00:22:54.820
typical to see families of nine to thirteen
children, and of course - but now Quebec has

00:22:54.820 --> 00:22:56.990
the lowest birth rate, I believe, in the Western
world.

00:22:56.990 --> 00:22:58.550
It’s way below replacement.

00:22:58.550 --> 00:23:00.179
Everybody bailed out of the church.

00:23:00.179 --> 00:23:04.490
Nobody gets married, and if you were a separatist
- if you were a lapsed Catholic, you were

00:23:04.490 --> 00:23:07.160
ten times more likely to be a separatist.

00:23:07.160 --> 00:23:12.780
All that meant was that when Catholicism fell
apart, you know, people who still needed to

00:23:12.780 --> 00:23:18.080
have very structured belief systems just turned
to Nationalism as a natural alternative, and

00:23:18.080 --> 00:23:25.380
that's part of what a -- that’s part of
what accounted for the rigidity and utopian…

00:23:25.380 --> 00:23:31.300
the utopian nature of the Quebec movement
towards independence.

00:23:31.300 --> 00:23:37.029
You know, I remember talking to one of my
colleagues, very, very intelligent person,

00:23:37.029 --> 00:23:42.620
and you know, I asked him because at that
point, they were predicting that if Quebec

00:23:42.620 --> 00:23:47.250
separated, the Canadian dollar would fall
to forty cents, forty-five cents US, something

00:23:47.250 --> 00:23:48.250
like that.

00:23:48.250 --> 00:23:49.800
It’d be a complete economic catastrophe.

00:23:49.800 --> 00:23:55.539
I said, well you know the predictions are
that if Canada separates, or Quebec separates

00:23:55.539 --> 00:23:59.429
and no one would know how to do that, is that
the Canadian economy will collapse, and of

00:23:59.429 --> 00:24:04.070
course, that'll collapse the Quebec economy
too, and he didn't deny that.

00:24:04.070 --> 00:24:06.429
He said, yeah but it would be worth it.

00:24:06.429 --> 00:24:10.200
And I thought, well there's just no way of
having a conversation under those circumstances

00:24:10.200 --> 00:24:15.380
because from my perspective, total, you know,
severe economic collapse is a good reason

00:24:15.380 --> 00:24:22.750
not to do something, but if you believed that
the future potential is such that that’s

00:24:22.750 --> 00:24:25.280
justified, then well there's no arguing with
you.

00:24:25.280 --> 00:24:28.820
It’s just something that decided, and that's
the end of that.

00:24:28.820 --> 00:24:32.941
When I lived in Quebec, as I did for a long
time, I learned very quickly never to have

00:24:32.941 --> 00:24:36.990
a discussion about politics with anyone who
was a separatist because it was just absolutely

00:24:36.990 --> 00:24:38.899
counterproductive, you know.

00:24:38.899 --> 00:24:44.750
They had axioms of belief that weren't movable,
like the future will be good enough so that

00:24:44.750 --> 00:24:48.100
no matter what price we pay in the present,
that will be justified.

00:24:48.100 --> 00:24:49.760
It’s like, well that's not an idea right.

00:24:49.760 --> 00:24:55.900
It’s a statement of faith, and you saw exactly
the same thing happening, not with the same

00:24:55.900 --> 00:25:00.250
principles, I’m not saying that, but you
saw the same thing happening from a psychological

00:25:00.250 --> 00:25:05.930
perspective in Russia when the Communists
really started to become active in the 1920s

00:25:05.930 --> 00:25:11.149
when any matter of horror whatsoever was fully
justifiable because it was going to bring

00:25:11.149 --> 00:25:16.890
about some future state that was basically
equivalent to paradise.

00:25:16.890 --> 00:25:23.429
So anyways, and then you know, Dostoevsky
pursued the idea of nihilism even farther

00:25:23.429 --> 00:25:26.450
because Dostoevsky was certainly someone who
was willing to go to the ends of an argument,

00:25:26.450 --> 00:25:33.570
and one of the things he proclaimed was that
if there was no god, then anything was permitted,

00:25:33.570 --> 00:25:39.710
and his basic hypothesis was well if there's
no ultimate arbiter of values, if there's

00:25:39.710 --> 00:25:44.970
no transcendent arbiter of values, then you're
radically free.

00:25:44.970 --> 00:25:49.299
Now, you know, the existentialists would say
you could use that radical freedom to find

00:25:49.299 --> 00:25:54.080
meaning in your life, but one of the things
Dostoevsky realized was that you could use

00:25:54.080 --> 00:25:56.820
that radical freedom for anything that you
wanted.

00:25:56.820 --> 00:26:02.760
So in his book “Crime and Punishment,”
for example, he explores the actions and beliefs

00:26:02.760 --> 00:26:07.529
of a student who was named Raskolnikov, and
Raskolnikov is a starving student.

00:26:07.529 --> 00:26:12.350
He’s a law student, and he doesn't have
enough to eat so of course his cognition is

00:26:12.350 --> 00:26:17.220
a little bit on the addled side because he’s
going through periods of starvation and drunkenness,

00:26:17.220 --> 00:26:19.610
so it’s not like he’s thinking that clearly.

00:26:19.610 --> 00:26:24.130
He wants to become a law student so that he
can help society, and he finds out that his

00:26:24.130 --> 00:26:30.210
sister is basically willing to enter into
a loveless marriage and more or less prostitute

00:26:30.210 --> 00:26:35.200
herself so that she can generate enough money
to share with her mother so that they can

00:26:35.200 --> 00:26:40.450
fund his continuation through law school,
and he thinks that wouldn't be a very good

00:26:40.450 --> 00:26:41.450
deal.

00:26:41.450 --> 00:26:46.710
At the same time, he’s indebted to a pawnbroker
who everyone hates, who’s an absolutely

00:26:46.710 --> 00:26:52.140
miserable person in every possible way, and
Dostoevsky sets up the situation like that,

00:26:52.140 --> 00:26:57.649
so the pawnbroker has a niece, if I remember
correctly who she basically enslaves and mistreats,

00:26:57.649 --> 00:27:02.450
and she squirrels away all sorts of money
but never does anything with it, lives in

00:27:02.450 --> 00:27:08.649
absolute poverty, and anyways, he considers
her the sort of person without whom the world

00:27:08.649 --> 00:27:10.470
would be a better place.

00:27:10.470 --> 00:27:17.001
So having all these things co-occurring in
his imagination, he decides that because there

00:27:17.001 --> 00:27:22.590
are no ultimate arbiters of value, that all
morality is essentially cowardice which is

00:27:22.590 --> 00:27:25.870
kind of the reverse of what nietzsche concluded.

00:27:25.870 --> 00:27:31.340
He said most people were cowardly and justified
that with their morality, but Raskolnikov

00:27:31.340 --> 00:27:36.140
took the other idea which is “Well why do
I have to obey any rules at all.

00:27:36.140 --> 00:27:41.840
If there’s no ultimate source for all of
these rules, it’s just convention and cowardice,

00:27:41.840 --> 00:27:45.850
and if I have enough strength then I could
leap outside of that framework and I can do

00:27:45.850 --> 00:27:47.000
whatever I want.”

00:27:47.000 --> 00:27:52.630
So he decides to kill the pawnbroker which
he does, and quite successfully, and not only

00:27:52.630 --> 00:27:56.730
that, he gets away with it, and that's about
the first third of “Crime and Punishment,”

00:27:56.730 --> 00:28:02.730
and the rest of the book is the discussion
of the manner in which he comes unglued as

00:28:02.730 --> 00:28:08.639
a consequence of having performed this act,
and it’s a brilliant - it’s an absolutely

00:28:08.639 --> 00:28:15.750
brilliant study of the way that a value system
holds you together even in ways that you don't

00:28:15.750 --> 00:28:21.769
know, and then if you step outside of that
and violate your relationship to it in some

00:28:21.769 --> 00:28:27.299
really intense way, then there's going to
be catastrophic psychological consequences

00:28:27.299 --> 00:28:29.440
that’ll echo through your whole being.

00:28:29.440 --> 00:28:35.940
We know that this is more than theory because
many people, soldiers in particular who develop

00:28:35.940 --> 00:28:40.770
Post Traumatic Stress Disorder, develop Post
Traumatic Stress Disorder when they observe

00:28:40.770 --> 00:28:46.700
themselves doing something that they regard
as tremendously cruel, or vicious, or immoral,

00:28:46.700 --> 00:28:54.440
you know, and they're in this situation where,
you know, acting in that manner is highly

00:28:54.440 --> 00:28:55.440
probable.

00:28:55.440 --> 00:28:56.440
It’s a war situation.

00:28:56.440 --> 00:29:00.169
It’s usually very intense, and you know,
they don't have a lot of time to make decisions,

00:29:00.169 --> 00:29:06.710
and god only knows, you know, what the specifics
are of the particular event, but many, many

00:29:06.710 --> 00:29:13.050
people come to be so shattered by observing
themselves do things they can't believe that

00:29:13.050 --> 00:29:17.470
a human being would be capable of doing that
they never recover, and it’s partly because,

00:29:17.470 --> 00:29:24.720
you know, that they violate their ethical
-- the ethical structure that holds them together,

00:29:24.720 --> 00:29:29.900
holds all their ideas, and their plans, and
their perceptions, and their interactions

00:29:29.900 --> 00:29:30.920
with other people.

00:29:30.920 --> 00:29:35.690
That's a unifying field in some sense, and
if you violate it and it fragments, then you're

00:29:35.690 --> 00:29:41.220
left absolutely fragmented and that's not
-- that's not even a psychological observation,

00:29:41.220 --> 00:29:43.850
it’s a psychophysiological observation.

00:29:43.850 --> 00:29:49.320
You can stress yourself so badly, raise your
stress levels so high that your brain starts

00:29:49.320 --> 00:29:55.700
to become damaged by the stress hormone and,
you know, so it’s not just a psychological

00:29:55.700 --> 00:30:01.590
state, it’s damage to the core of your being
in some sense.

00:30:01.590 --> 00:30:08.919
Now, the collapse of these belief systems
and their destabilization was well thought

00:30:08.919 --> 00:30:13.710
through by these thinkers by the end of the
nineteenth century, and then we have the technological

00:30:13.710 --> 00:30:19.860
transformations, and the sociological and
political transformations of the early part

00:30:19.860 --> 00:30:25.529
of the twentieth century that leaves everyone
in a state of confusion, in some sense, like

00:30:25.529 --> 00:30:26.899
in Germany in the 1920’s.

00:30:26.899 --> 00:30:33.049
Of course, the Germans had gone through this
terrible period of trench warfare, so all

00:30:33.049 --> 00:30:34.460
their men were brutalized.

00:30:34.460 --> 00:30:38.120
Some of them had been on the front for months,
and that was in the trenches, and then, you

00:30:38.120 --> 00:30:42.750
know, their political system had collapsed
and they put a weakly rooted democracy in

00:30:42.750 --> 00:30:46.909
place, and the economic system collapsed and
Germany went through a period of hyperinflation

00:30:46.909 --> 00:30:52.330
so that the value of their money basically
dropped to zero.

00:30:52.330 --> 00:30:57.010
So that meant if you were 65 years old, and
saved up your whole life to have enough money

00:30:57.010 --> 00:31:02.259
to retire, and you were a good citizen, you
know, and prudent and careful, every single

00:31:02.259 --> 00:31:08.830
thing you ever owned disappeared, and so at
the same time, you know, the Communist revolution

00:31:08.830 --> 00:31:13.740
had taken place in Soviet Russia, and there
was great concern within Germany that the

00:31:13.740 --> 00:31:18.909
same thing was likely to happen there, and
certainly the Communists were always pushing

00:31:18.909 --> 00:31:23.289
for that because they had the common term,
which is the international Communist movement

00:31:23.289 --> 00:31:29.950
which was devoted to destabilizing, you know,
non-Communist governments and producing the

00:31:29.950 --> 00:31:34.509
preconditions for the revolution, so it wasn't
like it was just paranoia, it was a real threat,

00:31:34.509 --> 00:31:39.190
and it was out of all that mess that came
the emergence of the fascists, and the Nazis,

00:31:39.190 --> 00:31:44.860
and it’s not that, you know, it’s not
that surprising because chaos breeds the desire

00:31:44.860 --> 00:31:46.639
for order.

00:31:46.639 --> 00:31:53.029
So that was one direction that people could
go, you know, instead of following some abstract

00:31:53.029 --> 00:31:58.269
Messiah, let’s say, the sort of idea that
was embedded in classical Christianity, they

00:31:58.269 --> 00:32:03.710
realized a new Messiah, and that was Hitler,
and that certainly didn't seem to be any improvement,

00:32:03.710 --> 00:32:09.590
you know, because Hitler was really a messiah
of destruction and fire, and you know, the

00:32:09.590 --> 00:32:13.429
World War II killed about a hundred and twenty
million people, and of course, it left Germany

00:32:13.429 --> 00:32:18.130
in absolute ruins, and Hitler killed himself
in his bunker underneath Berlin while it was

00:32:18.130 --> 00:32:22.539
burning and the Russians were advancing into,
you know, into Germany, and they were not

00:32:22.539 --> 00:32:23.539
happy.

00:32:23.539 --> 00:32:28.669
You didn't want to be a German national while
the Soviets were advancing towards you after

00:32:28.669 --> 00:32:34.139
your country had invaded theirs, pushed them
back halfway across the Soviet Union, you

00:32:34.139 --> 00:32:38.259
know, and produced a tremendous amount of
damage and distress.

00:32:38.259 --> 00:32:46.570
It was an awful situation, you know, and the
messiahs that the Russians turned to: Lenin,

00:32:46.570 --> 00:32:52.620
and Stalin were barbaric and brutal beyond
comprehension, and you know it’s a strange

00:32:52.620 --> 00:32:58.659
thing, you know, we’re not very well educated
in what happened in the Soviet Union in the

00:32:58.659 --> 00:32:59.659
twentieth century.

00:32:59.659 --> 00:33:04.070
We know far more about what happened in Germany,
say with the Holocaust, and you know, it’s

00:33:04.070 --> 00:33:08.679
very important that people should know about
that, but there were tens of millions of people

00:33:08.679 --> 00:33:14.529
brutally destroyed in the Soviet Union, and
you know, partly because they were murdered

00:33:14.529 --> 00:33:21.830
by people who were, at least in principle,
motivated by left-wing utopian visions.

00:33:21.830 --> 00:33:27.340
We seem to… our education system seems to
regard that as somehow more forgivable which

00:33:27.340 --> 00:33:29.830
it certainly isn't.

00:33:29.830 --> 00:33:37.389
So, you know, the whole generate secular alternatives
to religious belief issue didn't seem to work

00:33:37.389 --> 00:33:43.750
out very well, and then the nihilism alternative
-- well that has its own problems, you know.

00:33:43.750 --> 00:33:48.850
One problem is the sort that Dostoevsky talked
about, you know, and you see the kids who

00:33:48.850 --> 00:33:54.529
go up and shoot up high schools and explode
in rage, you know.

00:33:54.529 --> 00:33:58.289
They're often people who feel that they have
no meaning in their life, that life itself

00:33:58.289 --> 00:34:04.909
is contemptible and that suffering is too
extreme and that they bear the brunt of unfair

00:34:04.909 --> 00:34:13.460
reality, and you know, they develop unbelievably
dark and destructive theories of revenge and

00:34:13.460 --> 00:34:17.730
mayhem over, sometimes over the period of
years and then they go out in the world and

00:34:17.730 --> 00:34:24.010
lay those things out, and it’s not like
their thinking is irrational, you know.

00:34:24.010 --> 00:34:25.010
It’s coherent.

00:34:25.010 --> 00:34:28.940
It’s just predicated on principles that
you might not agree with, such as, you know,

00:34:28.940 --> 00:34:34.000
the principle that “everyone I don’t like
deserves to die,” but you know, in the absence

00:34:34.000 --> 00:34:42.079
of a really formal way of demonstrating that
such thoughts are not only immoral, but wrong,

00:34:42.079 --> 00:34:45.909
you know, in some absolute sense, it’s very
difficult to come up with ways of defending

00:34:45.909 --> 00:34:48.929
ourselves against those two extremes, you
know.

00:34:48.929 --> 00:34:54.730
The extreme of destructive nihilism, and the
extremes of ideological possession.

00:34:54.730 --> 00:35:01.930
Now, Nietzsche started to work out some solutions
to this, and I just started to touch on those

00:35:01.930 --> 00:35:06.290
at the end of the last lecture.

00:35:06.290 --> 00:35:11.079
You know, Dostoevsky’s solution was a return
to Christianity, fundamentally, and its revivification,

00:35:11.079 --> 00:35:17.440
and that was the same tact that Alexander
Solzhenitsyn attempted to lay out, and also

00:35:17.440 --> 00:35:23.391
Tolstoy in Russia, you know, and I’ll talk
to you about that a little bit more in the

00:35:23.391 --> 00:35:24.391
next lecture.

00:35:24.391 --> 00:35:26.980
Dostoevsky -- or Nietzsche’s idea was that
people would have to… he said, you know,

00:35:26.980 --> 00:35:31.849
in his quote about the death of God, that
people would have to become like gods just

00:35:31.849 --> 00:35:39.350
to be able to tolerate the consequences of
this dismemberment of the previous civilization,

00:35:39.350 --> 00:35:45.829
and he believed, well his thinking on this,
I would say, is somewhat fragmentary.

00:35:45.829 --> 00:35:52.640
I mean, Nietzsche was a great critic of Christianity,
institutionalized Christianity, and a great

00:35:52.640 --> 00:35:53.640
diagnostician.

00:35:53.640 --> 00:36:00.040
He could say what was rotten at the core of
Western civilization, we’ll say modern civilization,

00:36:00.040 --> 00:36:05.950
but when it came to actually describing what
to do about it, well he didn't live that long,

00:36:05.950 --> 00:36:06.950
you know.

00:36:06.950 --> 00:36:07.950
He died a fairly young man.

00:36:07.950 --> 00:36:11.109
There's actually a video of Nietzsche in the
mental hospital online.

00:36:11.109 --> 00:36:12.510
I just found it the other night.

00:36:12.510 --> 00:36:17.369
I had no idea that he was ever captured on
video, but there's video from -- movie from

00:36:17.369 --> 00:36:23.390
about 1899 showing him in a mental hospital
where he ended up in his early forties.

00:36:23.390 --> 00:36:30.630
So he talked about the development of the
being he called the overman, which is often

00:36:30.630 --> 00:36:37.910
translated as superman, and his idea was that
people would have to take onto themselves

00:36:37.910 --> 00:36:44.440
the burden of creating new systems of meaning
and new moralities that were suited to them,

00:36:44.440 --> 00:36:47.690
that they would have to create new values.

00:36:47.690 --> 00:36:57.420
Now, Jung took Nietzsche’s diagnosis very,
very seriously, and you can certainly say

00:36:57.420 --> 00:37:02.150
that Jung was as much influenced by Nietzsche
as he was by Freud, and I would say in some

00:37:02.150 --> 00:37:07.070
ways, he was influenced more because one of
the things that Jung was trying to do was

00:37:07.070 --> 00:37:11.119
to identify where the loss values had gone.

00:37:11.119 --> 00:37:18.290
So the Nietzschean idea is that it’s possible
for human beings to create their own values.

00:37:18.290 --> 00:37:24.240
Now, there's a problem with that, and there's
a variety of problems with that, and one is

00:37:24.240 --> 00:37:31.470
that it doesn't seem exactly true in that,
you know, and a lot of the existentialists

00:37:31.470 --> 00:37:35.819
who followed Nietzsche, like Jean-Paul Sartre
for example, believed in the radical freedom

00:37:35.819 --> 00:37:43.020
of human beings, that we were doomed to be
free, in a sense, and it was absolutely necessary

00:37:43.020 --> 00:37:47.630
for us to conjure up our own meanings and
values because fundamentally, we face the

00:37:47.630 --> 00:37:53.480
void and, you know, life was nasty, and brutish,
and short, to use Hobbes’s terms, and that

00:37:53.480 --> 00:38:00.210
we had to be able to confront that and live
despite it, and well, there's a variety of

00:38:00.210 --> 00:38:02.060
problems with that solution.

00:38:02.060 --> 00:38:06.099
The first one is, well, if you come up with
your systems of values, there's no reason

00:38:06.099 --> 00:38:11.130
-- and I come up with mine, there’s no reason
to assume that they're going to be sufficiently

00:38:11.130 --> 00:38:15.040
integrable so that we don't have to fight
each other to the death.

00:38:15.040 --> 00:38:16.040
That's a big problem.

00:38:16.040 --> 00:38:19.820
So, you know, you have every single person
with their own system of beliefs.

00:38:19.820 --> 00:38:26.310
Well, fine, except how do -- in a sense, that's
a structured, kind of philosophical anarchy.

00:38:26.310 --> 00:38:30.730
Well, okay, maybe that's good if you happen
to live alone on an island, but if you're

00:38:30.730 --> 00:38:35.540
stuck with all these other people, then that
becomes a very difficult thing to manage,

00:38:35.540 --> 00:38:40.859
and I think it’s partly for that reason
that Sartre famously said, “Hell is other

00:38:40.859 --> 00:38:46.240
people,” you know, because he thought of
the other, in some sense, that which was not

00:38:46.240 --> 00:38:51.420
him, as a suppressive force that stopped him
from manifesting his destiny in the way that

00:38:51.420 --> 00:38:53.970
would have been best for him.

00:38:53.970 --> 00:38:59.580
Well, you know, hell might be other people,
but that doesn't mean that -- first of all

00:38:59.580 --> 00:39:03.780
that's a very one-sided way of looking at
things because, of course, hell is other people.

00:39:03.780 --> 00:39:06.960
Hell is you too, and you know, hell is nature.

00:39:06.960 --> 00:39:11.829
There's lots of places that you can find hell,
but by the same token, you know, the most

00:39:11.829 --> 00:39:16.670
meaningful elements of people’s lives are
often in their social relationships, and you

00:39:16.670 --> 00:39:21.380
can't lay everything at the door of pathological
society, you know, and we shouldn't forget

00:39:21.380 --> 00:39:25.160
as well that Sartre didn’t…

00:39:25.160 --> 00:39:31.309
what -- come up with any reasonable critique
of the Communists until the late 1960s, and

00:39:31.309 --> 00:39:33.069
you know, that was a little late.

00:39:33.069 --> 00:39:38.319
People with any sense, like George Orwell,
had figured out that the whole Soviet experiment

00:39:38.319 --> 00:39:46.330
had become radically murderous by the early
1940s, and so Sartre, you know, proposed radical

00:39:46.330 --> 00:39:54.680
freedom as a… as the existential response
to the unveiling of no meaning, but when it

00:39:54.680 --> 00:39:59.579
came right down to it, he couldn't resist
identification with a totalitarian structure,

00:39:59.579 --> 00:40:04.589
so I don't see any reason why we should really
pay any attention to what he had to say.

00:40:04.589 --> 00:40:08.800
So… and now there's another problem with
the idea that people should create their own

00:40:08.800 --> 00:40:14.819
values, and that is that it’s not so simple,
you know, and the problem seemed to be that

00:40:14.819 --> 00:40:17.359
you don't obey yourself very well.

00:40:17.359 --> 00:40:19.750
You know, you can say, “Well here’s my
code.

00:40:19.750 --> 00:40:21.160
I’m going to live by it.”

00:40:21.160 --> 00:40:23.690
So, and let’s do that simply to begin with.

00:40:23.690 --> 00:40:28.960
You say, “Well I’m going to study very
hard and do well at my classes,” just for

00:40:28.960 --> 00:40:32.520
the sake of argument, you know, but you don't,
you know.

00:40:32.520 --> 00:40:36.089
You procrastinate, and you have a paper you're
supposed to read, and you know you're supposed

00:40:36.089 --> 00:40:40.560
to read it, and you need to read it for reasons
that are hypothetically important to you,

00:40:40.560 --> 00:40:43.940
but there's no damn way you can get yourself
to sit down and read it.

00:40:43.940 --> 00:40:48.770
Your attention wanders, and you go to do three
or four stupid things, and you feel terrible

00:40:48.770 --> 00:40:53.300
about it, like you feel like you're betraying
yourself, and maybe this is a continual pattern

00:40:53.300 --> 00:40:57.670
in your life, but one of the things you find
out is that you don't get to create your own

00:40:57.670 --> 00:41:02.790
damn values because, for some reason, you're
not in charge of yourself, and of course that's

00:41:02.790 --> 00:41:06.900
where the psychoanalytic idea started to come
from, you know, Freud notices -- this is in

00:41:06.900 --> 00:41:11.880
the aftermath of Nietzsche -- that you're
not the master of your own psyche, you know,

00:41:11.880 --> 00:41:17.930
that there's many sub-yous inside of you and
that they don't all want the same thing, and

00:41:17.930 --> 00:41:24.220
so the idea that you can generate your own
meaning is very -- it’s an insufficiently

00:41:24.220 --> 00:41:33.430
developed idea because there's a lot of meaning-making
generators residing within you, and not only

00:41:33.430 --> 00:41:38.329
do they not all point in the same direction
which is a huge problem, but they don't even

00:41:38.329 --> 00:41:43.309
necessarily -- they don't necessarily lay
themselves out in some integrated fashion

00:41:43.309 --> 00:41:48.500
across time, and they don't necessarily operate
together in a way that's going to enable you

00:41:48.500 --> 00:41:52.060
to find your place with other people and in
society.

00:41:52.060 --> 00:41:54.859
So, you know, make your own meaning.

00:41:54.859 --> 00:41:56.830
Well, which part of you, you know?

00:41:56.830 --> 00:41:58.800
You're not a unified thing.

00:41:58.800 --> 00:42:04.319
So that's a big problem, and then well these
other problems just remain unaddressed completely.

00:42:04.319 --> 00:42:09.950
Well, one answer to that, and this is the
answer that the more radical existentialists

00:42:09.950 --> 00:42:14.869
took is that well, society has to be reconfigured,
but you know, we kind of know where that leads

00:42:14.869 --> 00:42:15.869
too.

00:42:15.869 --> 00:42:21.400
When people are doing radical societal reconfiguration,
at least as far as we’ve been able to tell,

00:42:21.400 --> 00:42:25.740
most of the time that's an absolute, murderous,
catastrophe.

00:42:25.740 --> 00:42:31.089
So, you know, it’s reasonable -- it seems
reasonable to me to presume that those experiments

00:42:31.089 --> 00:42:33.470
have already been run.

00:42:33.470 --> 00:42:42.390
Alright so, now Jung, like Dostoevsky, was
very interested in returning to sources of

00:42:42.390 --> 00:42:48.480
meaning from which he believed that our original
religious ideas had emerged, and this is partly

00:42:48.480 --> 00:42:50.730
his notion of the collective unconscious.

00:42:50.730 --> 00:42:57.200
So, you know, part of the radical critique
of religious systems is predicated on the

00:42:57.200 --> 00:43:02.940
idea that there's something like conscious
beliefs, you know, there are articulated beliefs

00:43:02.940 --> 00:43:06.670
that you could lay out in a credo, but that's
not right.

00:43:06.670 --> 00:43:10.541
It doesn't seem to be correct at all, and
Nietzsche actually knew this, you know.

00:43:10.541 --> 00:43:14.960
He knew that a lot of our social institutions
had emerged from the bottom up and had only

00:43:14.960 --> 00:43:20.260
become articulated after they had been embodied
and danced out essentially, you know.

00:43:20.260 --> 00:43:26.020
You -- a tribal group learns how to organize
itself over thousands and thousands of years

00:43:26.020 --> 00:43:30.359
of trial and error and pushing against each
other, and so forth, and they come into that

00:43:30.359 --> 00:43:36.970
tribal grouping with a biological substrate,
and their sociological and political interactions

00:43:36.970 --> 00:43:41.380
are constrained by all of those things, and
then maybe they come up with a description

00:43:41.380 --> 00:43:46.500
of that, over time, a self-description, and
an articulated representation, and that's

00:43:46.500 --> 00:43:47.940
the religious system.

00:43:47.940 --> 00:43:54.280
It’s not that the religious system is thought
out first, as a system of metaphysical presuppositions,

00:43:54.280 --> 00:44:00.420
images, and dreams, then turned into rules,
then imposed on the population who then obeys

00:44:00.420 --> 00:44:08.119
it, and generally speaking, when people criticize
formal religion, they criticized it assuming

00:44:08.119 --> 00:44:12.059
that that's how it developed, you know, and
that's kind of a Marxist idea, for example,

00:44:12.059 --> 00:44:16.740
that religion is the opiate of the masses,
and that you know, the religious structures

00:44:16.740 --> 00:44:20.599
were laid out so that a small elite could
control the population.

00:44:20.599 --> 00:44:28.500
Now, you know, in virtually every domain,
a small elite emerges that dominates the population.

00:44:28.500 --> 00:44:30.220
I don't care what domain you look at.

00:44:30.220 --> 00:44:37.580
So Marx is accurate in that way, in that,
you know, there's always a power imbalance

00:44:37.580 --> 00:44:45.950
between elite minority and a non-elite majority,
but to say that that's the cause of all the

00:44:45.950 --> 00:44:53.180
systems that people interact within is, well
it’s unsophisticated beyond belief.

00:44:53.180 --> 00:44:57.640
What it does is that it takes phenomena that
are complex beyond comprehension and reduce

00:44:57.640 --> 00:44:58.640
them to one thing.

00:44:58.640 --> 00:45:05.410
It’s not helpful, like you know, here’s
an example.

00:45:05.410 --> 00:45:13.109
If you sampled popular songs on YouTube, let’s
say you made a graph of how popular songs

00:45:13.109 --> 00:45:14.140
were.

00:45:14.140 --> 00:45:22.010
What you find is that about ten songs, at
any given time, are played -- half of all

00:45:22.010 --> 00:45:26.599
the songs played at any given time are going
to be one of ten songs.

00:45:26.599 --> 00:45:30.920
Well, and then what the other thing you’d
find is that half of all songs played are

00:45:30.920 --> 00:45:37.260
played by one or more of ten musicians, and
that's true no matter if you look all the

00:45:37.260 --> 00:45:41.890
from the 1930s to now, if you look at popular
music, you see the same thing.

00:45:41.890 --> 00:45:47.559
Almost everything that almost everyone listens
to is created by very few people, and then

00:45:47.559 --> 00:45:52.300
there's millions of musicians, but you've
never heard of most of them and you'll never

00:45:52.300 --> 00:45:53.960
hear a song from most of them.

00:45:53.960 --> 00:45:59.089
It’s a small minority, and what you see
is in any field of creative production, this

00:45:59.089 --> 00:46:00.089
happens.

00:46:00.089 --> 00:46:04.750
A small elite emerges, and dominates the entire
landscape.

00:46:04.750 --> 00:46:11.940
Now, you know, it would be kind of ridiculous
to assume that the ten most popular singers

00:46:11.940 --> 00:46:16.540
and musicians that are currently operative
were those who gave rise to the system that

00:46:16.540 --> 00:46:17.930
allowed them to thrive.

00:46:17.930 --> 00:46:24.420
I mean, obviously that's a dopey idea, and
it’s no more an intelligent idea when you

00:46:24.420 --> 00:46:29.920
look at any other domain where there's tremendous
variation, and the emergence of an elite.

00:46:29.920 --> 00:46:32.050
It’s a very common phenomena.

00:46:32.050 --> 00:46:36.119
It happens, as I said, it happens anywhere
there's creative production, and so that's

00:46:36.119 --> 00:46:39.770
why one percent of the people, you know, roughly
have fifty percent of the money.

00:46:39.770 --> 00:46:43.470
It’s no different in other creative domains.

00:46:43.470 --> 00:46:48.520
Now, we’ll talk later in the course about
why that happens, but to think it’s because

00:46:48.520 --> 00:46:54.270
those people set up the system so that they
could thrive is -- well there’s an element

00:46:54.270 --> 00:47:00.030
of that, obviously, because once you're rich,
you're going to prefer political policies

00:47:00.030 --> 00:47:04.400
that help you stay rich, but that doesn't
mean you set up the whole damn system that

00:47:04.400 --> 00:47:09.880
made you rich to begin with, plus it’s not
the same people over any reasonable span of

00:47:09.880 --> 00:47:10.880
time.

00:47:10.880 --> 00:47:14.950
So, the one percent of people who have most
of the money, it’s always one percent, but

00:47:14.950 --> 00:47:17.720
it’s not the same people, you know.

00:47:17.720 --> 00:47:22.359
Each individuals tend to hold on to money
for very short periods of time, and big companies

00:47:22.359 --> 00:47:24.290
don't last very long, you know.

00:47:24.290 --> 00:47:29.170
They last on average about 30, 35, 40 years,
and that's it, then they disappear.

00:47:29.170 --> 00:47:33.980
So, and it’s because, you know, the economic
landscape is just churning like mad.

00:47:33.980 --> 00:47:39.670
It’s very difficult for a company to -- you
know, there's not that many big steam coach

00:47:39.670 --> 00:47:46.380
companies anymore, you know, and nobody make
zeppelins, and nobody makes typewriters.

00:47:46.380 --> 00:47:50.040
You know what I mean, it’s things move quick,
and just because you dominated the landscape

00:47:50.040 --> 00:47:55.089
at one particular moment doesn't mean you're
going to be able to do it at the next.

00:47:55.089 --> 00:47:59.859
Alright, so the create your own meaning thing
is a rough one, and then there's another problem

00:47:59.859 --> 00:48:04.299
too which is “What makes you think you have
enough time?”

00:48:04.299 --> 00:48:09.890
You know, lots of times people come to me
and they have relationship problems, and part

00:48:09.890 --> 00:48:16.140
of the problem is that they've set their relationships
up outside of social norms, and they do that,

00:48:16.140 --> 00:48:20.380
so they'll say something to me like well we’re
not going to get married because marriage

00:48:20.380 --> 00:48:24.839
is just a piece of paper, which is really
a stupid thing to say, like it’s an incredibly

00:48:24.839 --> 00:48:30.010
stupid thing to say, but underneath that,
there's this idea that they want to remain

00:48:30.010 --> 00:48:34.270
free of social constraints so that they can
negotiate their own way, like if you're giving

00:48:34.270 --> 00:48:38.319
them credit, for you know, wanting freedom
instead of just escaping responsibility, but

00:48:38.319 --> 00:48:41.750
the problem with that is, it’s like okay,
good luck.

00:48:41.750 --> 00:48:43.320
Try it.

00:48:43.320 --> 00:48:48.280
I don't know why you would assume that you
have enough time in the thirty or forty years

00:48:48.280 --> 00:48:53.270
that you're going to be pursuing relationships
to actually figure out how they should run.

00:48:53.270 --> 00:48:58.140
You don't have a hope of that, and it’s
worse too because very, very few people can

00:48:58.140 --> 00:49:03.470
negotiate, you know, because here's the way
it works.

00:49:03.470 --> 00:49:08.520
You either adhere to the social order, or
you stand outside it.

00:49:08.520 --> 00:49:13.309
As soon as you stand outside of it, you're
in a chaotic place because there's no guidelines,

00:49:13.309 --> 00:49:19.300
and then you either live chaotically because
there's no guidelines or you start to formulate

00:49:19.300 --> 00:49:26.160
order, but to do that, you have to know what
you want, and you have to know how to express

00:49:26.160 --> 00:49:31.579
it, and then you have to figure out what your
partner wants, and then help them express

00:49:31.579 --> 00:49:34.740
it and then you have to negotiate a solution.

00:49:34.740 --> 00:49:39.670
Well, I would say one in twenty people know
how to negotiate.

00:49:39.670 --> 00:49:41.050
It’s really, really difficult.

00:49:41.050 --> 00:49:43.230
I mean, just think of the steps.

00:49:43.230 --> 00:49:46.590
First of all, you have to know what you want,
and then you have to admit it to yourself.

00:49:46.590 --> 00:49:52.160
Well, yeah right, like you're not even gonna
get to the first one in all likelihood.

00:49:52.160 --> 00:49:53.250
What do you want?

00:49:53.250 --> 00:49:56.589
A lot of what you want can't be articulated
even, you know.

00:49:56.589 --> 00:49:58.990
I’ll give you an example.

00:49:58.990 --> 00:50:06.660
So, there's a great study done awhile back
on prediction of relationship longevity.

00:50:06.660 --> 00:50:10.320
Okay, so here was the question.

00:50:10.320 --> 00:50:16.730
“How many negative interactions do you have
to have, per set of positive” -- sorry.

00:50:16.730 --> 00:50:20.990
“How many positive interactions, per negative
interaction, do you have to have with your

00:50:20.990 --> 00:50:25.020
partner in order for the relationship to remain
stable?”

00:50:25.020 --> 00:50:30.580
Okay, so let's say you have one negative interaction
to every one positive interaction.

00:50:30.580 --> 00:50:34.990
Okay, or maybe you have ten negatives to every
positive, then you can imagine a different

00:50:34.990 --> 00:50:39.309
situation where you have a hundred positives
to one negative, right, spanning the whole

00:50:39.309 --> 00:50:44.549
potential continuum, and you use that to predict
relationship satisfaction and longevity.

00:50:44.549 --> 00:50:50.490
Well, you might think, well god obviously,
a hundred positive to one negative is where

00:50:50.490 --> 00:50:55.780
the preferable ratio and so it’s those people
who, you know, their relationship is nothing

00:50:55.780 --> 00:50:58.780
but constant compliments and bliss.

00:50:58.780 --> 00:50:59.780
They're the ones who last.

00:50:59.780 --> 00:51:01.819
It’s not true.

00:51:01.819 --> 00:51:08.799
What you see is that there's an optimal…
an optimal ratio domain.

00:51:08.799 --> 00:51:14.549
If it falls below five to one positive to
negative, then your relationship falls apart.

00:51:14.549 --> 00:51:19.320
It’s too negative, and it’s partly because
people feel negative emotion more than they

00:51:19.320 --> 00:51:25.410
feel positive emotion because you can be hurt
more than you can be pleased, and so one that's

00:51:25.410 --> 00:51:30.820
only five to one is too punishing, and people
won't stay in it, but if you get above to

00:51:30.820 --> 00:51:37.200
eleven to one, it gets not punishing enough,
and then you think well what does that mean

00:51:37.200 --> 00:51:38.200
exactly?

00:51:38.200 --> 00:51:40.170
Well, what do you want in a relationship?

00:51:40.170 --> 00:51:41.880
Well you think, bliss.

00:51:41.880 --> 00:51:44.750
It’s like, that isn't what you want, as
it turns out.

00:51:44.750 --> 00:51:47.740
It’s more like you want someone to contend
with, you know.

00:51:47.740 --> 00:51:49.250
You don't want a pushover.

00:51:49.250 --> 00:51:55.011
You don't want everything just to be easy,
you know, and this is the sort of… the sort

00:51:55.011 --> 00:51:58.319
of phenomena that Kierkegaard was talking
about when he talked about deciding to make

00:51:58.319 --> 00:52:01.660
things more difficult for people because that's
what they need.

00:52:01.660 --> 00:52:06.050
You know, you know this perfectly well because
if you go outside with someone and they worship

00:52:06.050 --> 00:52:10.400
you, and they dolt on your every word, and
there's nothing but positive feedback coming

00:52:10.400 --> 00:52:14.400
from them, you lose respect for them almost
instantly, and you go wander off and find

00:52:14.400 --> 00:52:19.490
someone whos more interesting, and part of
the reason for that, I think, is that you

00:52:19.490 --> 00:52:25.060
want the person that you're with to challenge
you so that not only do you do reasonably

00:52:25.060 --> 00:52:30.619
well day to day together, you know, so that
you can co-exist in the same space with a

00:52:30.619 --> 00:52:35.230
reasonable amount of peace, but you also want
there to be enough tension in the relationship

00:52:35.230 --> 00:52:39.480
so that you're both involved in a process
of mutual transformation.

00:52:39.480 --> 00:52:43.640
Well, try specifying that in an articulated
way, you know.

00:52:43.640 --> 00:52:44.839
Good luck.

00:52:44.839 --> 00:52:49.060
You know, and it also explains strange things
about people like the fact that they'll stay

00:52:49.060 --> 00:52:53.540
in pretty negative relationships, like what
the hell are you doing there?

00:52:53.540 --> 00:52:57.559
If you’d articulated it two years ago, and
you said, “Well I want to be with someone

00:52:57.559 --> 00:53:01.849
I’m miserable with half the time,” of
course, you're never going to say that, but

00:53:01.849 --> 00:53:04.980
it could easily be that that's what you're
after.

00:53:04.980 --> 00:53:16.369
So well...so alright now, Heidegger is another
philosopher who was attempting in some sense

00:53:16.369 --> 00:53:20.650
to solve the problems that were laid out by
Nietzsche and Dostoevsky, and the way that

00:53:20.650 --> 00:53:30.150
Heidegger began to resolve them was by taking
a radically new look at philosophy itself,

00:53:30.150 --> 00:53:38.400
and he was one of the prime phenomenologists,
and I told you awhile back that the phenomenologists

00:53:38.400 --> 00:53:45.420
decided to reconstitute Western philosophy
so that it was focusing on being instead of

00:53:45.420 --> 00:53:50.359
knowledge, and so the hardest thing to grasp
about the phenomenologists is what exactly

00:53:50.359 --> 00:53:54.270
they meant by being, and -- so I’ll give
you a…

00:53:54.270 --> 00:53:56.630
I’ll give you an overview of that.

00:53:56.630 --> 00:54:00.329
So that's where the term “da sein” comes
from, and that's a German term, and it means

00:54:00.329 --> 00:54:01.329
“being there.”

00:54:01.329 --> 00:54:06.970
So right now, you're encapsulated in a da
sein, and the da sein is the totality of your

00:54:06.970 --> 00:54:14.820
experience, and that experience would be an
experience of an extended world, the natural

00:54:14.820 --> 00:54:21.670
world, and then the social world, and then
inside that, the world of your subjective

00:54:21.670 --> 00:54:27.930
experience, and that constitutes being, and
the phenomenologists make the case -- they're

00:54:27.930 --> 00:54:32.670
not playing the subject-object game, they're
standing outside the division between subject

00:54:32.670 --> 00:54:35.770
and object that's part of the scientific worldview.

00:54:35.770 --> 00:54:41.280
So it’s a real paradigm shift in that you
can't use the rules from the old way of looking

00:54:41.280 --> 00:54:43.910
at things inside the new way of looking at
things.

00:54:43.910 --> 00:54:46.530
You have to start with new presuppositions.

00:54:46.530 --> 00:54:52.140
So we might say, well, one of the things that
you're going to do if you look at things phenomenologically

00:54:52.140 --> 00:54:56.500
is to assume that everything that you experience
is real.

00:54:56.500 --> 00:55:03.670
So then, we would say that there's no attempt,
in a phenomenological world, to reduce pain

00:55:03.670 --> 00:55:05.260
to something material.

00:55:05.260 --> 00:55:09.150
Pain stands as -- stands itself as a phenomena.

00:55:09.150 --> 00:55:10.690
So does anxiety.

00:55:10.690 --> 00:55:12.000
So does joy.

00:55:12.000 --> 00:55:19.530
All the things that the scientists of consciousness
call qualia, which are viewed by them as qualities

00:55:19.530 --> 00:55:23.049
of the objective world, aren't viewed that
way by the phenomenologists.

00:55:23.049 --> 00:55:27.250
They just say those are primary elements of
being.

00:55:27.250 --> 00:55:31.140
So -- and it’s a very interesting way of
looking at things because it kind of allows

00:55:31.140 --> 00:55:34.420
you to reclaim the validity of your own experience.

00:55:34.420 --> 00:55:37.510
You can no longer say, “well that only subjective.”

00:55:37.510 --> 00:55:45.039
Now that doesn't mean that everything you
claim subjectively is true, objectively or

00:55:45.039 --> 00:55:46.059
for other people.

00:55:46.059 --> 00:55:51.720
What it does mean is that everything that
you experience subjectively is real.

00:55:51.720 --> 00:55:55.880
Now that doesn't mean you have to not think
about it or take it apart or categorize it

00:55:55.880 --> 00:56:03.299
properly, you still have to do all of those
things, but you're put into a place where

00:56:03.299 --> 00:56:08.089
there's no need to deny the reality of your
own experience, or to subordinate it to something

00:56:08.089 --> 00:56:09.089
else.

00:56:09.089 --> 00:56:16.300
So, for example, if I’m doing dream analysis
with someone, which I do often, if people

00:56:16.300 --> 00:56:23.380
dream, because dreams, as Jung pointed out,
are… they're manifestations of being.

00:56:23.380 --> 00:56:24.780
You don't come up with them.

00:56:24.780 --> 00:56:25.980
They appear to you.

00:56:25.980 --> 00:56:28.930
They sort of appear out of nowhere, in some
sense.

00:56:28.930 --> 00:56:30.849
They manifest themselves.

00:56:30.849 --> 00:56:36.789
They do it strangely, and I think the reason
for that is that they contain unarticulated

00:56:36.789 --> 00:56:43.150
thought, but if you can get a handle on them
and assess them, then sometimes they can tell

00:56:43.150 --> 00:56:46.770
you things that there's no other way you can
figure out, and what's really cool about them

00:56:46.770 --> 00:56:57.140
is that they're -- they have the same personality
as phenomena in the, like the broader world

00:56:57.140 --> 00:56:58.700
of experience have, you know.

00:56:58.700 --> 00:57:01.460
You don't ever think about the truth of a
chair.

00:57:01.460 --> 00:57:04.430
It’s just there, and dreams are like that.

00:57:04.430 --> 00:57:09.829
They're just there, and if you can untangle
what they have to -- what they indicate, then

00:57:09.829 --> 00:57:21.839
you can get a take on your own experience
that's not altered by any of your local subjective

00:57:21.839 --> 00:57:22.839
wishes and desires.

00:57:22.839 --> 00:57:28.180
I hate to use the word subjective in this
sort of context.

00:57:28.180 --> 00:57:31.440
So, phenomenology is the study of being.

00:57:31.440 --> 00:57:37.099
Now, and -- in being, there are variety of
aspects, and so one aspect -- I’ve never

00:57:37.099 --> 00:57:42.190
remembered the names of these, but I’ll
get it here right away.

00:57:42.190 --> 00:57:43.190
Oh yes.

00:57:43.190 --> 00:57:47.380
Heidegger broke the world of experience, being,
into three basic categories.

00:57:47.380 --> 00:57:54.130
There was the umwelt, which I think is basically
the world beyond culture and the individual.

00:57:54.130 --> 00:57:59.180
There's the mitwelt, and that's the world
that we share with everyone else, so that's

00:57:59.180 --> 00:58:03.819
roughly the social world and the social structures,
and then there's the eigenwelt which is that

00:58:03.819 --> 00:58:08.460
domain of experience that's unique to you,
that other people can't partake in.

00:58:08.460 --> 00:58:15.299
So those are the elements of being, as far
as the phenomenologists were concerned, and

00:58:15.299 --> 00:58:21.099
in those domains of being, different experiences
manifest themselves.

00:58:21.099 --> 00:58:30.280
We talked about some of the ones that would
be manifestations of the eigenwelt.

00:58:30.280 --> 00:58:33.660
Scientists would call those things emotional
or motivational states.

00:58:33.660 --> 00:58:36.000
Normally, people think of them as feelings.

00:58:36.000 --> 00:58:38.240
I would say, “I feel thus.

00:58:38.240 --> 00:58:45.329
I feel such and such,” and those are experiences
that manifest themselves to you or that you

00:58:45.329 --> 00:58:51.970
have, depending on how you look at it, that
are indicative of the manner in which you

00:58:51.970 --> 00:58:54.920
act in relationship to being.

00:58:54.920 --> 00:59:01.349
Now, one of -- part of the reason that this
is relevant to psychotherapy in particular

00:59:01.349 --> 00:59:08.600
is because the phenomenologists were very
interested in the manifestation of meaning.

00:59:08.600 --> 00:59:16.450
So you could say, well, nihilism is the absence
of meaning, and totalitarianism is the fixedness

00:59:16.450 --> 00:59:21.650
of meaning, right, if you're a totalitarian,
what you do is say, “All meanings exist

00:59:21.650 --> 00:59:23.539
in relationship to this structure.”

00:59:23.539 --> 00:59:29.630
It’s almost as if -- the phenomenologists
would say you're trying to reduce the umwelt,

00:59:29.630 --> 00:59:35.720
which is the natural world, the mitwelt, which
is the social world, and the eigenwelt, which

00:59:35.720 --> 00:59:40.650
is your own world, you're trying to reduce
all of that to the mitwelt so that everything

00:59:40.650 --> 00:59:47.000
falls under an explanation that's granted
to you by some higher authority, and then

00:59:47.000 --> 00:59:50.210
of course, the nihilists are having none of
that.

00:59:50.210 --> 00:59:58.690
They see -- they use their eigenwelt, I would
say, their own world to invalidate meaning

00:59:58.690 --> 01:00:00.910
in any domain.

01:00:00.910 --> 01:00:07.619
Now the phenomenologists would say, well it’s
a mistake to use your rationality to undermine

01:00:07.619 --> 01:00:12.640
the sense, the manifestation of meaning, and
I can give you an example of that.

01:00:12.640 --> 01:00:17.869
So let’s say you're a good nihilist, and
you think maybe you're going to go do something

01:00:17.869 --> 01:00:23.579
difficult like put yourself through university,
and then you think in a relatively depressed

01:00:23.579 --> 01:00:28.700
state of mind, maybe you encounter some obstacles
of one form or another, and you think, “Oh

01:00:28.700 --> 01:00:30.000
to hell with this.

01:00:30.000 --> 01:00:32.180
Who’s -- What difference does it make anyways?

01:00:32.180 --> 01:00:34.560
Who cares if I go and get my degree, you know.

01:00:34.560 --> 01:00:38.420
None of this knowledge is particularly relevant
or meaningful and who the hell’s gonna know

01:00:38.420 --> 01:00:40.230
the difference in a million years?”

01:00:40.230 --> 01:00:47.380
And so you think, well that's a perfectly
rational dismissal because who is gonna know

01:00:47.380 --> 01:00:52.160
in a million years or let’s say, well even
if you can make case that someone might know,

01:00:52.160 --> 01:00:56.530
there might be some effects left of you in
a million years, then we’ll just multiply

01:00:56.530 --> 01:00:59.309
it by a hundred thousand and go to a trillion
years.

01:00:59.309 --> 01:01:03.970
So here you are, you're this little tiny speck
on a slightly bigger speck in the middle of

01:01:03.970 --> 01:01:09.190
a galaxy that has god only knows how many
billion stars, and then there's a billion

01:01:09.190 --> 01:01:13.140
of those galaxies, although there's way more
than that, and they're spread across this

01:01:13.140 --> 01:01:19.150
tremendous expanse of time, and in the face
of all that, who cares what you do?

01:01:19.150 --> 01:01:28.170
Well, what a phenomenologist would say is,
okay, let’s look at how meaning manifests

01:01:28.170 --> 01:01:37.980
itself when you alter your own private world
in a variety of manners.

01:01:37.980 --> 01:01:42.880
So let’s say, you're trying to do something
-- maybe you're working in a -- maybe you're

01:01:42.880 --> 01:01:46.720
working as a volunteer in a hospital helping
sick kids, you know.

01:01:46.720 --> 01:01:52.339
You're reading to them so that they're distracted
from their pain, and you say well, in a trillion

01:01:52.339 --> 01:01:56.099
years, who’s gonna know the difference and
so you think, well it’s meaningless to do

01:01:56.099 --> 01:02:01.430
this, and the phenomenologists would say,
if the frame of reference that you're using,

01:02:01.430 --> 01:02:09.240
like if you're imposing -- if you're transforming
the way your being manifests itself so that

01:02:09.240 --> 01:02:16.349
it becomes meaningless and absurd, then you
should try experiencing it in a different

01:02:16.349 --> 01:02:17.349
manner.

01:02:17.349 --> 01:02:22.431
So, it’s a -- see, because a rationalist
in some sense, the phenomenologists would

01:02:22.431 --> 01:02:27.200
say, a rationalist can't deal with the argument
“What difference is it going to make in

01:02:27.200 --> 01:02:30.880
a trillion years, and here you are, a little
dust speck among all these other dust specks.

01:02:30.880 --> 01:02:40.099
It’s ultimately meaningless.”A phenomenologist
would say, “Maximize the meaning.

01:02:40.099 --> 01:02:41.339
That's the marker of truth.”

01:02:41.339 --> 01:02:45.030
It’s a completely different way of thinking
about it.

01:02:45.030 --> 01:02:50.200
So he would say, for example, that if you're
going to a hospital and you're reading to

01:02:50.200 --> 01:02:55.220
sick children, that the frame of reference
that you should use, the way that you allow

01:02:55.220 --> 01:03:02.020
that experience to manifest itself, should
be such that the experience manifests itself

01:03:02.020 --> 01:03:06.799
so that it’s as meaningful as possible rather
than as meaningless.

01:03:06.799 --> 01:03:17.240
The idea being that just because you can twist
your own experience so that certain elements

01:03:17.240 --> 01:03:22.900
of your being become meaningless does not
mean that that’s right.

01:03:22.900 --> 01:03:28.049
The fact that it becomes meaningless actually
means that it’s wrong because you think,

01:03:28.049 --> 01:03:34.760
you see, it all depends on what you allow
to be primary, and this is the phenomenologist

01:03:34.760 --> 01:03:35.760
point.

01:03:35.760 --> 01:03:40.890
If you allow your strict rationality to be
primary, then if it can attack something and

01:03:40.890 --> 01:03:45.580
destroy it, then that thing is worthy of being
attacked and destroyed.

01:03:45.580 --> 01:03:52.030
But if you flip it around and say, well what
you should be doing is allowing -- is interacting

01:03:52.030 --> 01:03:57.540
with your experience or allowing your experience
to manifest itself in a manner that situates

01:03:57.540 --> 01:04:05.099
you most meaningfully in the here and now,
whatever framework you're using to do that

01:04:05.099 --> 01:04:06.450
that works is right.

01:04:06.450 --> 01:04:10.559
Well it’s a completely different way of
looking at things and it’s a real -- it’s

01:04:10.559 --> 01:04:18.099
a real escape from the pathology of rationalism,
you know, because it isn't obvious that what

01:04:18.099 --> 01:04:20.569
you think should take priority.

01:04:20.569 --> 01:04:23.430
Now, the phenomenologists would go farther
than that.

01:04:23.430 --> 01:04:37.039
They would say that -- and this is something…
so here's a way of thinking about it.

01:04:37.039 --> 01:04:42.320
So Binswanger said, “What we perceive are
first and foremost” -- Binswanger was a

01:04:42.320 --> 01:04:46.339
psychiatrist who was very much influenced
-- Binswanger and Boss are the people we’re

01:04:46.339 --> 01:04:49.859
going to talk about mostly -- were very much
influenced by Heideggerian ideas.

01:04:49.859 --> 01:04:55.890
They say, “What we perceive are first and
foremost not impressions of taste, tone, smell,

01:04:55.890 --> 01:05:00.490
or touch, not even things or objects, but
meanings.”

01:05:00.490 --> 01:05:06.109
Okay, so the idea for the phenomenologists
is that what being is made out of is meaning.

01:05:06.109 --> 01:05:10.760
It isn't that the objective world is made
out of things, it’s that being is made out

01:05:10.760 --> 01:05:11.760
of meaning.

01:05:11.760 --> 01:05:15.119
Now some of those meanings can be positive,
and some of them can be negative, and some

01:05:15.119 --> 01:05:19.980
of them can be neutral, but the fundamental
constituent elements of being -- the fundamental

01:05:19.980 --> 01:05:25.770
constituent element of being is meaning, and
then there is an argument between Binswanger

01:05:25.770 --> 01:05:28.500
and Boss about how that meaning manifests
itself.

01:05:28.500 --> 01:05:32.710
So Binswanger would say that, “You endow
meaning on the world.”

01:05:32.710 --> 01:05:37.170
So that's kind of a Nietzschean idea, that
you create your own values so that you have

01:05:37.170 --> 01:05:42.060
within you something that he called an a priori
ontological structure.

01:05:42.060 --> 01:05:46.920
It’s a world designer, a matrix of meaning,
that determines how the world manifests itself

01:05:46.920 --> 01:05:47.920
to you.

01:05:47.920 --> 01:05:52.720
Now, the easiest way to think about that is
that, you know, you're thrown into a particular

01:05:52.720 --> 01:05:57.550
time and place -- that's another existential
idea and that's part of the absurdity of your

01:05:57.550 --> 01:06:02.060
life, is that you're here, now, in this particular
context and situation.

01:06:02.060 --> 01:06:05.369
It’s something you have to contend with,
and that's true for everyone.

01:06:05.369 --> 01:06:10.119
There are arbitrary preconditions to everyone’s
being, and one of those arbitrary preconditions

01:06:10.119 --> 01:06:15.470
is the structure through which you look at
the world, and that structure enables some

01:06:15.470 --> 01:06:21.250
things to be highlights and some things to
be ignored, and so, the way that meaning manifests

01:06:21.250 --> 01:06:27.730
itself is a reflection of this a priori ontological
structure, the a priori mode of being.

01:06:27.730 --> 01:06:33.740
So, there can't be being, which is to say
your experience, for the sake of argument,

01:06:33.740 --> 01:06:41.011
without the structure that consists of -- that
you consist of, and so that's a given, and

01:06:41.011 --> 01:06:45.349
it’s the action of that structure that determines
the meaning of things.

01:06:45.349 --> 01:06:48.490
Now, Boss would say exactly the opposite.

01:06:48.490 --> 01:06:52.589
He would say, that's the wrong way of looking
at it.

01:06:52.589 --> 01:06:59.250
You should look at the totality of your existence,
which is partly the broad natural world, the

01:06:59.250 --> 01:07:03.619
cultural world, and your own world, and you
should note that meaning arises in different

01:07:03.619 --> 01:07:05.900
places of its own accord.

01:07:05.900 --> 01:07:12.990
You can't -- you can't reduce it to the action,
say, of this ontological a priori, ontological

01:07:12.990 --> 01:07:18.630
structure, and so one example would be, well
what about the meaning of things you don't

01:07:18.630 --> 01:07:19.630
understand?

01:07:19.630 --> 01:07:25.150
Well it’s hard to understand how the meaning
of things you don't understand can be attributed

01:07:25.150 --> 01:07:27.140
to what you understand.

01:07:27.140 --> 01:07:29.609
The meaning seems to be there, to begin with.

01:07:29.609 --> 01:07:31.960
So here's an example.

01:07:31.960 --> 01:07:36.859
You're - you have a relationship with someone,
and you discover that they have an affair.

01:07:36.859 --> 01:07:41.250
Well, the discovery of the affair is going
to be something that's going to be meaningful.

01:07:41.250 --> 01:07:43.069
Now, you don't know what the meaning is.

01:07:43.069 --> 01:07:47.799
You're going to interpret it to begin with,
likely very negatively, except to the degree

01:07:47.799 --> 01:07:52.420
that part of you would like to escape out
of the relationship, right, because sometimes

01:07:52.420 --> 01:07:56.410
if you're betrayed, you're happy about it
because it’s time for that to be over with.

01:07:56.410 --> 01:08:00.920
In any case, there are things that you can
encounter that you don't understand that are

01:08:00.920 --> 01:08:03.080
meaningful in and of themselves.

01:08:03.080 --> 01:08:11.240
So, I think that you actually can't separate
your structure from the structure of everything.

01:08:11.240 --> 01:08:16.260
They’re always interacting, and meaning
emerges out of the interplay of them.

01:08:16.260 --> 01:08:19.150
So, here’s another way of thinking about
it.

01:08:19.150 --> 01:08:21.160
You're reading a book.

01:08:21.160 --> 01:08:23.040
The book is meaningful.

01:08:23.040 --> 01:08:24.160
Where is the meaning?

01:08:24.160 --> 01:08:26.680
Is it in your head?

01:08:26.680 --> 01:08:28.490
Is it in the book?

01:08:28.490 --> 01:08:33.990
Well, it’s very difficult to say, right,
because obviously there's a subjective element

01:08:33.990 --> 01:08:34.990
to it.

01:08:34.990 --> 01:08:40.720
There's an element that's unique to you, but
just as obviously, that meaning wouldn't manifest

01:08:40.720 --> 01:08:45.340
itself if it wasn't for the book, and of course
the book wouldn't be meaningful if you and

01:08:45.340 --> 01:08:49.930
the book weren't embedded in this complex
structure, and so the meaning is an emergent

01:08:49.930 --> 01:08:55.950
property -- the meaning is an emergent property
of the interplay of all of the elements of

01:08:55.950 --> 01:08:57.850
being.

01:08:57.850 --> 01:09:01.120
That's a very interesting way of looking at
things, so you have all these elements of

01:09:01.120 --> 01:09:05.470
being, and their dance produces meaning of
one form or another.

01:09:05.470 --> 01:09:10.530
Now, you might say, if you were a phenomenologist,
that some of that meaning is going to be life

01:09:10.530 --> 01:09:15.630
sustaining, and some of it is going to be
life destroying, and the phenomenologist would

01:09:15.630 --> 01:09:23.080
say from a clinical perspective, that you’re
-- if you’re… if you exist in a system

01:09:23.080 --> 01:09:27.950
of meaning revelations that are life destroying,
that you should turn your attention away from

01:09:27.950 --> 01:09:34.330
them towards meanings that are life affirming,
and you know, one of the things that’s quite

01:09:34.330 --> 01:09:38.350
interesting about the phenomenological perspective
is that you could experiment with it quite

01:09:38.350 --> 01:09:39.350
easily.

01:09:39.350 --> 01:09:44.110
So I could say to you, for example, a couple
of phenomenological experiments, one would

01:09:44.110 --> 01:09:51.950
be, for the next two weeks, you want to detach
yourself in some sense, so that you're a curious

01:09:51.950 --> 01:09:53.430
observer of your being.

01:09:53.430 --> 01:10:00.020
You're not necessarily trying to direct it,
you know, you're just trying to let it unfold,

01:10:00.020 --> 01:10:07.340
and then what you might want to watch for
is when the meaning that manifests itself

01:10:07.340 --> 01:10:15.610
as things flow around you is clearly meaning
of the life affirming type.

01:10:15.610 --> 01:10:19.620
Now you’ll see that, you know, it depends
on how well situated you are in some sense

01:10:19.620 --> 01:10:27.350
because if you're -- if you're surrounded
by -- if your experiential field is primarily

01:10:27.350 --> 01:10:31.750
negative, these are going to be relatively
rare events, but they will still not be non-existent.

01:10:31.750 --> 01:10:36.200
That might only happen for a few seconds,
or a few minutes everyday, or every two days,

01:10:36.200 --> 01:10:41.110
but so say, okay you notice all of a sudden
that you're in a place where things are the

01:10:41.110 --> 01:10:42.830
way you would like them to be.

01:10:42.830 --> 01:10:47.430
So you could say you're in a place where being
is manifesting itself as acceptable.

01:10:47.430 --> 01:10:51.750
Okay, so that's a place where nihilism is
not appropriate.

01:10:51.750 --> 01:10:56.630
It doesn't apply because the quality of the
experience is such that it’s life affirming.

01:10:56.630 --> 01:10:58.120
You have to notice that.

01:10:58.120 --> 01:11:03.260
It’s something that happens, and then you
might ask yourself, well okay, what are the

01:11:03.260 --> 01:11:09.970
preconditions that enable that -- what are
the preconditions that enabled all of the

01:11:09.970 --> 01:11:16.330
different subelements of being to work harmoniously
together at that time and place so that that

01:11:16.330 --> 01:11:18.560
was the meaning that emerged?

01:11:18.560 --> 01:11:24.370
And then the next question might be, how would
it be possible for me to allow that to happen

01:11:24.370 --> 01:11:26.450
more frequently?

01:11:26.450 --> 01:11:32.200
And so you can tilt yourself towards life
affirming meanings and away from… from,

01:11:32.200 --> 01:11:36.890
say, meanings that are associated with despair
and nihilism, but you do that partly -- it’s

01:11:36.890 --> 01:11:38.890
almost like you're navigating in a boat.

01:11:38.890 --> 01:11:40.260
In fact, I think you are.

01:11:40.260 --> 01:11:45.230
I think you're navigating to find the line
between order and chaos because that's where

01:11:45.230 --> 01:11:46.230
the meaning is.

01:11:46.230 --> 01:11:50.070
That's exactly where the meaning is, and you
could feel it like it’s a place, and that's

01:11:50.070 --> 01:11:53.410
the other thing that phenomenologists are
trying to get across.

01:11:53.410 --> 01:11:55.570
These things are real.

01:11:55.570 --> 01:11:58.890
They're not secondary manifestations of some
deeper reality.

01:11:58.890 --> 01:12:00.840
They are reality itself.

01:12:00.840 --> 01:12:04.850
I also think, and we’ll talk about this
more when we get to the neuropsychological

01:12:04.850 --> 01:12:10.100
portion of the course, that your brain is
actually set up first and foremost to detect

01:12:10.100 --> 01:12:11.100
meaning.

01:12:11.100 --> 01:12:18.310
You detect meaning before you detect object,
and it’s partly because you have to detect

01:12:18.310 --> 01:12:23.070
meaning so that you know what to do when something
happens very rapidly, and there are times

01:12:23.070 --> 01:12:26.870
when you have to figure out what to do before
you have enough time to even see what’s

01:12:26.870 --> 01:12:27.870
there.

01:12:27.870 --> 01:12:32.010
You just don't have the time, and so you know,
part of the question is well, what exactly

01:12:32.010 --> 01:12:34.310
do you mean by meaning, you know.

01:12:34.310 --> 01:12:39.490
I think meaning is significance for behavior
or significance for the structure that governs

01:12:39.490 --> 01:12:44.510
behavior, but those are very, very basic fundamental
perceptions.

01:12:44.510 --> 01:12:47.520
They're not -- you see the object, and then
you derive the meaning.

01:12:47.520 --> 01:12:50.240
It’s exactly the opposite in many cases.

01:12:50.240 --> 01:12:55.510
You perceive the meaning and derive the object,
and there's plenty of neurophysiological evidence

01:12:55.510 --> 01:12:56.510
for that.

01:12:56.510 --> 01:13:03.300
For example, you have lots of, you know, your
retina is a pattern detector, and the retinal

01:13:03.300 --> 01:13:08.280
information is transmitted to your brain along
the optic nerve, but the optic nerve branches

01:13:08.280 --> 01:13:10.700
and it goes lots of places in your body.

01:13:10.700 --> 01:13:15.620
Some of it maps right onto your spinal cord,
so that your eyes can make your body move.

01:13:15.620 --> 01:13:20.180
Some of it maps onto your amygdala so that
what you perceive are the meanings of facial

01:13:20.180 --> 01:13:22.310
expressions, without even perceiving the face.

01:13:22.310 --> 01:13:26.990
So you can have people who have blindsight,
a damaged visual cortex, you can show them

01:13:26.990 --> 01:13:33.270
angry faces which they say they can't see,
but they'll respond to them electrophysiologically

01:13:33.270 --> 01:13:38.390
as if they're being exposed to something negative,
and it’s because the retinal pattern is

01:13:38.390 --> 01:13:43.160
manifesting itself right onto the system that
maps one form of meaning.

01:13:43.160 --> 01:13:48.690
So you can clearly have meaning without object
perception, and so the idea that you derive

01:13:48.690 --> 01:13:52.730
the meaning from the object -- you see the
thing, and then you attribute meaning to the

01:13:52.730 --> 01:13:59.760
thing -- that's right at some levels of analysis,
but it’s wrong at many other levels of analysis.

01:13:59.760 --> 01:14:07.120
And so, the other thing that I think that
you can try that’s phenomenologically informed.

01:14:07.120 --> 01:14:09.200
This is quite an interesting trick.

01:14:09.200 --> 01:14:12.940
It’s really hard on you though, so be careful
if you try this.

01:14:12.940 --> 01:14:17.080
So, one other thing that you can try for two
weeks is to watch what you say.

01:14:17.080 --> 01:14:19.570
You got to detach yourself again.

01:14:19.570 --> 01:14:30.290
You have to remove the belief that your thoughts
and you are the same thing, and then you have

01:14:30.290 --> 01:14:36.850
to watch mostly what you say, and then you
have to see -- and this is something that

01:14:36.850 --> 01:14:41.780
Rogers would also be an advocate of -- you
have to see if what you say makes you feel

01:14:41.780 --> 01:14:48.730
-- you have to see if what you say improves
the quality of your being or makes it worse,

01:14:48.730 --> 01:14:53.700
and you know, if you stop believing in what
you say and watch what the consequences are

01:14:53.700 --> 01:14:59.140
instead, how it manifests itself in terms
of a transformation of being, then you can

01:14:59.140 --> 01:15:03.750
also learn how to only say things that improve
the quality of being.

01:15:03.750 --> 01:15:09.810
Well, that's a good thing if you can manage
it, but it’s terrifying in some sense because

01:15:09.810 --> 01:15:14.540
one of the things that you’ll find is that
hardly anything that you say does that.

01:15:14.540 --> 01:15:18.950
Most of it is neutral, but a fair bit of it
has exactly the opposite consequence.

01:15:18.950 --> 01:15:22.250
It makes things worse.

01:15:22.250 --> 01:15:29.190
So, I think I mentioned that the word phenomena
-- that's where the phenomenology, the term

01:15:29.190 --> 01:15:31.060
phenomenology comes from, obviously.

01:15:31.060 --> 01:15:36.060
The term phenomena means -- it’s from the
Greek word, “phainesthai,” and phainesthai

01:15:36.060 --> 01:15:38.470
means “to shine forth.”

01:15:38.470 --> 01:15:46.280
And so, the phenomenologists’ argument is
that being is made up of meanings that shine

01:15:46.280 --> 01:15:49.640
forth for you and that those different meanings
attract you.

01:15:49.640 --> 01:15:52.640
They're like -- they're like guideposts that
you can follow.

01:15:52.640 --> 01:15:57.000
So you know, one of the -- I’ve noticed
this, for example, in relationship to reading.

01:15:57.000 --> 01:16:01.360
So I’ll be reading a complex text, and some
passage will really strike me.

01:16:01.360 --> 01:16:03.720
So it manifests itself as meaningful.

01:16:03.720 --> 01:16:05.200
Why is that?

01:16:05.200 --> 01:16:10.620
Well, you know, one of the phenomenologists,
which would be Binswanger, would say, well

01:16:10.620 --> 01:16:15.420
it’s because of the way that I’m structured,
but Boss would say, well no, it’s a dance

01:16:15.420 --> 01:16:22.650
that's occurring within the structure of everything
and the consequence of that is the manifestation

01:16:22.650 --> 01:16:23.740
of this meaning.

01:16:23.740 --> 01:16:29.640
Well, what I found is that I can follow threads
of meaning through books, you know, that it’s

01:16:29.640 --> 01:16:34.860
like it’s something that's guiding me, that
sense of meaning, and often if I find something

01:16:34.860 --> 01:16:39.210
within a book that's meaningful, then I’ll
read the other things that that person wrote,

01:16:39.210 --> 01:16:43.830
and I’ll see that some of the people that
they read have meaningful things to say, and

01:16:43.830 --> 01:16:48.670
then I can branch out that network, and it’s
following a pathway that's laid out for you,

01:16:48.670 --> 01:16:54.250
in some sense it’s laid out for you in the
world, and you know, the phenomenological

01:16:54.250 --> 01:17:01.600
idea is that if you follow that pathway, then
what happens is being becomes more and more

01:17:01.600 --> 01:17:10.600
integrated around you, and those experiences
of intrinsic meaning start to multiply and

01:17:10.600 --> 01:17:16.240
increase in intensity, and you know, if you're
able to do that over a long period of time,

01:17:16.240 --> 01:17:22.440
then you can get more and more of the totality
of your being revealing the kind of meanings

01:17:22.440 --> 01:17:26.480
that's stopped you from being either nihilistic
or totalitarian.

01:17:26.480 --> 01:17:32.780
And so, well, that's the basic -- that's the
fundamental theory of phenomenological psychotherapy,

01:17:32.780 --> 01:17:33.780
you know.

01:17:33.780 --> 01:17:37.420
You might first say, well you know, you're
depressed and anxious and your life isn't

01:17:37.420 --> 01:17:38.420
going very well.

01:17:38.420 --> 01:17:40.490
What keeps you afloat?

01:17:40.490 --> 01:17:48.350
It’s something to observe, and then we might
say, well are there ways that we can expand

01:17:48.350 --> 01:17:53.890
that small area that's keeping you afloat
so that it starts to occupy more and more

01:17:53.890 --> 01:17:59.570
of your experience and push back the parts
that are either neutral or negative, and you

01:17:59.570 --> 01:18:09.480
know, it’s a -- it’s in large part a consequence
of attention, and willingness to first of

01:18:09.480 --> 01:18:15.440
all, treat things as if their meaning is real,
and then second, to allow that reality to

01:18:15.440 --> 01:18:24.720
transform the way that you experience the
things in the future.

01:18:24.720 --> 01:18:25.410
See you Thursday.

